  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1202 AM CDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nhe weak mid-level remnants of Edoard (weak disturbance aloft) \nwill meander over the portion of Texas mostly east of I-35 and \nsouth of I- 20 on Thursday. This will be on the southwestern \nperiphery of an upper level ridge centered over the southeastern \nCONUS. Moisture will increase over our eastern and southeastern \ncounties Thursday and Friday. Precipitable water values are \nforecast to increase to between 1.6 and 1.9 inches across that \narea. While locally heavy rain will be possible with any showers \nand storms, the coverage of convection is expected to be low. At \nthis time, leaning on a scenario where the area mainly east of a \nBrownwood to Junction has a low chance (15-25 percent) for showers\nand storms Thursday through Friday. \n\nOtherwise, temperatures will remain hot Thursday with highs in the \nupper 90s to 101 degrees in our southern and east-central \n(Heartland) counties, and 100-104 across the Concho Valley and Big \nCountry. These will be near the record highs for Thursday (September \n3) which are 104 for San Angelo (set in 2000) and 105 for Abilene \n(set in 2023). With some increase in cloud cover and a slight \nweakening of the 850mb thermal ridge Friday and Saturday, highs will \ndrop back a few degrees but still be above normal. A large upper \nlevel high will become recentered over New Mexico and the Texas \nPanhandle Saturday and Sunday. The center of this high is then \nprogged to shift east over the southern Plains early next week, as\nan upper low and trough move northeast across the Pacific \nNorthwest and into Montana. With this pattern, expect a \ncontinuation of hot and dry conditions for our area with above \nnormal temperatures.",
